# General — Frontend

Refer to and follow this entire rule when working under `apps/web`. Do not skip sections.

Full examples for coding practices live in `docs/CODING_PRACTICES.md`. Follow that doc together with this rule.

## Communication

- Always return concise output only, unless explicitly asked to be verbose.
- Prefer pointed answers; skip restating the task, padding, or unnecessary explanation.
- After a UI change, say what changed and where to look — nothing more.

## Sibling apps

When editing the backend (`apps/server`), read `.cursor/rules/general-backend.mdc` **before** changing it. Frontend rules do not apply there; backend rules do.

## Package manager

This project uses **pnpm** as the primary package manager (`pnpm-lock.yaml` is the source of truth).

- Install, add, remove, and update packages with `pnpm` only (`pnpm install`, `pnpm add`, `pnpm remove`, `pnpm update`).
- Run project scripts with `pnpm` (`pnpm dev`, `pnpm build`, `pnpm lint`, `pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm format`).
- Do **not** use `npm` or `yarn` for installs or scripts unless the user explicitly asks.
- Do not create or commit `package-lock.json` or `yarn.lock`.

## Coding practices

See `docs/CODING_PRACTICES.md` for full examples of the practices below.

- **Props:** Always define a dedicated props type/interface (e.g. `ComponentNameProps`). Do **not** destructure in the function signature — use `props: ComponentNameProps`, then destructure inside the body.
- **SOLID:** Design components and modules with single responsibility, open/closed, and clear dependency boundaries.
- **Imports:** Prefer the `@` alias; avoid deep relative paths (`../../../`).
- **README naming:** Module- or feature-specific README files use **UPPERCASE_SNAKE_CASE** (e.g. `PAYMENT_FLOW_README.md`). Root stays `README.md`.
- **Extract sections:** For distinct UI sections, extract a helper component in the **same file** (below the main export) instead of long inline JSX.
- **Early returns:** Guard invalid inputs and edge cases at the top; avoid deep `if`/`else` nesting so the happy path stays linear.
- **`cn`:** Use `cn` from `@/lib/utils` for all non-trivial `className` composition (conditionals, variants, merging parent `className`). No string concatenation or template-literal class builds.
- **Exports:** Prefer **named exports**. Use `export default` only when the framework requires it (e.g. Next.js `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`).
- **Type files:** Do not use generic `types.ts` for feature modules. Use explicit names (e.g. `settlement-ledger-types.ts`).
- **Colocate helpers:** Keep single-use handlers/helpers next to the component that uses them. Lift only for clear reuse or cross-component dependency.

## Folder grouping / feature modules

Group related modules by concern into theme folders. Keep a file flat only if it stands alone; otherwise nest it with its siblings.

When a feature file grows (many distinct UI roles or ~300–400+ lines), turn it into a **module folder**:

- **Split by responsibility / UI role** (e.g. header, body, footer, dialog) — not into generic dump files like `types.ts` or `utils.ts`.
- **Prefix filenames and exports with the module name** so they stay clear next to sibling modules.
- **Colocate helpers** with the only consumer. Lift to shared only when **two or more** files need them.
- **Public types** live on the main entry (or with the feature that owns them); re-export from `index.ts`.
- **Imports:** always `@/…`, including inside the module. Callers import the folder entry (`@/…/module-name`), not deep internals, unless a specific piece is intentional.
- Keep a **stable public API** via `index.ts` so internal refactors do not churn call sites.

## Code safety

Prefer `enum` or `const` (e.g. `as const` objects / const maps) over magic strings and numbers where it adds type safety — status values, routes, roles, keys, and other fixed sets.

## Validation regexes

Put shared format regexes in `constants/regex/*.constant.ts`. Group by theme; do not create one file per pattern or inline the same regex across modules. Keep `apps/server` `src/constants/regex/` aligned when a format is shared.

## List tables — server pagination

API-backed tables: URL `page`/`size` (nuqs, 10/25/50) → list `*PageQuery` → `StyledTable` with `serverTotalRows={total}`. Pass API filters from the URL; keep filter options static/enums. No client slice of a full fetch and no fake `pageSize=100` on primary tables. Mock-only tables may stay client-paged.

## Dangerous actions — confirmation

- Destructive / hard-to-undo actions (deactivate, delete, wipe demo data) need an `AlertDialog` — never one-click.
- Deactivate / delete a named entity: typed confirm; disable until the phrase matches; clear on close.
- Deactivate phrase: `deactivate {name}`. Delete phrase: `{name}` (unless that flow already has a stronger phrase).
- Soft recoveries (reactivate) stay one-click. Lower-stakes danger (Reset demo) may use Cancel + confirm without typing.
- Reuse existing patterns in the codebase; do not invent a second confirm UI.

## Form fields — required + info

Every form field label must show:

- A required asterisk (`*`) when the field is required to continue or submit.
- An info control (icon + tooltip) when the field needs a short explanation of what to enter or how it is used.

Info (and field validation) copy lives under `constants/form-field-info/`, one file per form section. Shared copy that spans flows goes in `shared.ts`. Do not dump all forms into a single file. Use `as const` maps; no inline magic strings for field info. Pass the constant into the field’s `info` prop (or equivalent). Keep live validation messages (e.g. format errors) separate from info tooltips, in the same section file when they belong to that step.

## UI changes

For any UI change, read and follow the `emil-design-eng` skill (`.agents/skills/emil-design-eng/SKILL.md`) first, then proceed. Do not implement UI work without consulting it.

## Shadows

When a component needs lift or separation from the surface, prefer a **general-use subtle shadow** — e.g. Tailwind `shadow-xs` / `shadow-sm`, or the shadow already on the matching shadcn control — not heavy, stacked, or glow-like shadows.

- **Confirm with `emil-design-eng` first** before adding or changing any shadow. If the skill (or existing shadcn patterns) say a border/ring is enough, skip the shadow.
- Prefer inheriting shadows from `components/ui` primitives (dialog, popover, select, sheet, button) over inventing new shadow stacks on screen layout.
- Do not add shadows to dense table rows, every card by default, or high-frequency hover targets just for decoration.

## UI components — shadcn

Use shadcn/ui for interface controls. This project’s style is `base-vega` (`components.json`). Installed components live in `components/ui`.

- Before building a control, use the matching component in `components/ui`. If it is missing, add it with `pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add <name>`. Do not hand-roll a second version of a component shadcn already ships.
- Cover as much of the UI as those components allow: buttons, inputs, selects, date pickers, dialogs, sheets, dropdown menus, popovers, tooltips, badges, separators, and the rest of the registry.
- Dropdowns use `Select` (`components/ui/select`). Dates use `DatePicker` (`components/ui/date-picker`, built from Popover and Calendar).
- Compose screens from these components. Custom markup is for layout and for domain pieces the registry does not cover.

## Typography — numbers

When a UI element shows **only numbers** (counts, KPIs, currency amounts, IDs, quantities, percentages, tabular columns of figures), use **tabular nums** so digits align and don’t shift layout as values change.

- Prefer Tailwind `tabular-nums` (or `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`).
- Apply on the element that renders the number, not on mixed prose that happens to include a digit.
- Skip for incidental numbers inside sentences (e.g. “Order placed 2 days ago”).

## Number inputs

Number inputs must not show the browser stepper (the up/down arrows).

- Hide the spinner on every `input[type="number"]` in `app/globals.css` (`appearance: textfield` plus the WebKit spin-button pseudo-elements). Do not restyle it per field.
- Keep `type="number"` (and `inputMode`) so mobile keyboards stay numeric. Users change the value by typing.
- Do not add custom stepper buttons unless a flow explicitly needs them.

## Micro-interactions

Prefer **subtle** micro-interactions with the **`motion`** library (`motion/react` — Framer Motion). Do not invent a second animation stack or add `framer-motion` separately.

- Use motion for state-change feedback: press, hover settle, overlay enter/exit, step transitions, list item appear/dismiss.
- Skip animation for high-frequency actions (keyboard shortcuts, rapid toggles, dense table row hover). Prefer CSS for simple color/opacity hovers when Motion adds no value.
- Keep motion short and quiet (~120–220ms, ease-out or soft ease like `[0.23, 1, 0.32, 1]`). Avoid long, bouncy, or attention-seeking effects unless rare and intentional.
- Animate `opacity` / `transform`; avoid layout props unless necessary. Use `AnimatePresence` for mount/unmount with short exits.
- Always respect reduced motion via `useReducedMotion()` — skip or collapse to instant/opacity-only when preferred.
- Import from `motion/react`. For non-trivial motion design, follow the `animate` skill.

## Mock data loading — skeletons + delay

When a screen is a clickable mock (`localStorage` / seed data): instant paint looks unfinished; always show loading UI for mock screens.

- Use `useMockLoading()` from `hooks/use-mock-loading.ts` when that hook exists — random delay **500–1500ms** after client hydrate (`MOCK_LOAD_DELAY_MS`).
- Do **not** invent a second delay range or hard-code `setTimeout` for mock latency; extend that hook/constants if needed.
- **Tables**: pass `loading={useMockLoading()}` (or a shared parent `loading`) into `StyledTable` / `HeadlessTable` so the skeleton stays **inside** the table frame (toolbar + rows + pagination). Use `components/table/table-skeleton.tsx` — do not replace the table with a bare “Loading…” string.
- **Other surfaces** (stat cards, detail pages, setup gates): use `Skeleton` (`components/ui/skeleton.tsx`, fast shimmer) or `PageSkeleton` / `DetailPageSkeleton` (`components/page-skeleton.tsx`) for the same delay.
- When one screen owns several blocks (e.g. dashboard stats + table), drive **one** `useMockLoading()` and pass `loading` down so they resolve together.
- Toasts for mock mutations use **Sonner** (`toast` from `sonner`, `<Toaster />` in root layout via `components/ui/sonner.tsx`).

## Skip browser checks

Do not open the browser, take screenshots, or click through the app to verify a change. The user checks the UI themselves.

Do not start the dev server for a visual check.
